WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:09.660
MobileNet to V1 and V2 gave you a way to implement a neural network that is more computationally

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:10.660
efficient.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:17.260
But is there a way to tune MobileNet or some other architecture to your specific device?

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:22.660
So maybe you're implementing a computer vision algorithm for different brands of mobile

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:27.740
phones with different amounts of compute resources or for different edge devices.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:31.220
And if you have a little bit more computation, maybe you can have a slightly bigger neural

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:34.340
network and hopefully get a bit more accuracy.

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:39.180
Or if you are more computationally constrained, maybe you want a slightly smaller neural network

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:43.100
that runs a bit faster at the cost of a little bit of accuracy.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:49.140
So how can you automatically scale up or down neural networks for a particular device?

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:52.300
EfficientNet gives you a way to do so.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:58.420
Let's say you have a baseline neural network architecture where the input image has a certain

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:07.980
resolution R and your neural network has a certain depth and the layers has a certain width.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:14.660
The authors of the EfficientNet paper Ming-Sing Tan and my former PhD student Kwok-Lor observed

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:20.540
that the three things you could do to scale things up or down are you could use a high

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:28.770
resolution image, so a new image resolution R. And I don't know how to denote a high

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:30.730
resolution in a video like this.

00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:36.290
So I'm using this blue glow here to denote maybe a high resolution image.

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:39.970
Or you could make this network much deeper.

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:49.970
So you could vary D, the depth of the neural network, or you can make the layers wider.

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:55.250
So you can also vary the width of these layers.

00:01:55.250 --> 00:02:04.850
So the question is, given a particular computational budget, what's a good choice of R, D and W?

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:10.930
Or depending on the computational resources you have, you can also use compound scaling

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:16.690
where you might simultaneously scale up or simultaneously scale down the resolution of

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:20.370
the image and the depth and the width of the neural network.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:26.370
Now the tricky part is, if you want to scale up R, D and W, what's the rate at which you

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:28.210
could scale up each of these?

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:31.250
Should you double the resolution and leave depth and width the same?

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:34.770
Or maybe you should double the depth but leave the others the same?

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:39.610
Or increase resolution by 10%, increase depth by 50% and width by 20%?

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:46.290
What's the best trade-off between R, D and W to scale up or down your neural network

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:50.930
to get the best possible performance within your computational budget?

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:56.610
So if you're ever looking to adapt a neural network architecture for a particular device,

00:02:56.610 --> 00:03:01.690
look at one of the open source implementations of EfficientNet, which will help you to choose

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:05.530
a good trade-off between R, D and W.

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:06.530
So that's it.

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:11.290
With MobileNet, you've learned how to build more computationally efficient layers.

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:17.930
And with EfficientNet, you can also find a way to scale up or down these neural networks

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:22.190
based on the resources of a device you may be working on.

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:27.530
So with this, I hope you have the skills needed in order to build neural networks for

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:32.730
mobile devices and for embedded devices and for other devices where the computation and

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:34.610
the memory may be more limited.

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:38.450
I hope this will open up a lot of possible applications that you may now be able to

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:38.850
build.
